text:
About this lecture


At Hubbard Brook in New Hampshire, scientists have measured rain and stream chemistry since the 1960s. Acid deposition fell sharply after the 1970s, yet the streams recovered far more slowly than expected. The reason was underground: decades of acid had stripped calcium from the soil.
Today you will learn that:

soil holds cations on negatively charged surfaces, counted in charge (cmolc/kg);
acid meets finite inventories: carbonate, exchangeable cations, then aluminium;
equal pH does not mean equal buffering;
retention is not the same as transport.

Last lecture, treatment moved substances from water onto solids. Soil does the same, without an operator.

 Hubbard Brook Experimental Forest, New Hampshire, USA. Photo: Mariel Carr, CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons.


Start with the story. Hubbard Brook is a forested research catchment where the chemistry of rain and streams has been recorded for about sixty years. It is one of the places where acid rain was first documented in North America. When sulfur emissions were cut, many expected the streams to recover quickly. They did not. Long-term soil measurements showed why: the acid had been neutralised partly by pulling calcium and magnesium off the soil’s exchange sites, and that stock refills only slowly by weathering. Today we build the chemistry behind that observation. Connect to last week: in water treatment we deliberately transferred substances to solids. Soil solids do this continuously, and they can also give them back.

Carbonate: fast but finite


\mathrm{CaCO_3+2H^+\rightarrow Ca^{2+}+CO_2^*+H_2O}
As in the carbonate lecture, each mole of calcite neutralises 2 equivalents of acid at this endpoint.
Example: 0.0100 mol accessible CaCO₃ can consume 0.0200 mol H⁺.
But protection is not unlimited:

the inventory is used up, not regenerated;
grains coated with iron oxides or gypsum react slowly;
calcite in a different layer from the flow path does nothing.


 Stora Alvaret, Öland: thin soil on limestone bedrock. Photo: Bernt Fransson, CC BY-SA 4.0, Wikimedia Commons.


Carbonate-bearing soils, such as those on limestone in Gotland, Öland or Skåne, are well protected against acid deposition. The stoichiometry is the same as the liming calculation in the carbonate lecture, and exercise 3 uses it directly. The cautions matter in practice. A stoichiometric inventory is an upper limit on what could react. Real protection depends on contact: calcite grains can be armoured with coatings, and calcite deep in the profile does not help the water that flows through the upper horizons. Once the carbonate is gone it does not come back on human time scales. That is why liming of acidified soils and lakes needs to be repeated.

About this lecture


In December 2013, the town of Ronneby in southern Sweden closed one of its waterworks. Its groundwater contained PFAS, fluorinated compounds from firefighting foam used for years at a nearby air base training site. Residents had drunk the water for a long time, and blood tests later showed strongly raised PFAS levels.
How did a foam sprayed on the ground end up in people’s blood?
Today you will learn that:

molecular structure sets where a compound goes: water, solids, air or organisms;
ionisation changes that answer with pH;
persistence is a separate property, described by a rate;
disappearing from water is not destruction.


 Sailors test a firefighting foam (AFFF) hose on an aircraft carrier, 2016. Photo: US Navy, public domain, Wikimedia Commons.


Open with Ronneby. The drinking water came from a groundwater source near the Blekinge air base, where firefighting foam had been used in training. The foam contained per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances, PFAS, which seeped into the ground and reached the wells. The waterworks was closed when the contamination was discovered in late 2013, and later health studies of residents became an important source of knowledge about PFAS exposure. The photo shows the same kind of foam in a different setting. Link to last lecture: we followed acid and metals out of a waste heap. Today we follow organic molecules, and we ask two questions about each one: where does it go, and how long does it last?

About this lecture


In 1972 the United Nations held its first environment conference in Stockholm. Sweden’s own case study argued something new: sulfur emitted in other countries was acidifying Swedish lakes. The argument linked air chemistry, soils and water in one chain, and it led to the first international treaty on cross-border air pollution, in 1979.
Today we build that kind of argument ourselves, with every unit of the course:

one budget in equivalents from air to lake;
annual export is not a closed-water pH;
a sediment redox hypothesis tested against evidence;
an explanation is only as good as the test that could break it.


 Delegates to the UN Conference on the Human Environment cycle through Stockholm, 1972. Photo: Pressens Bild, public domain, Wikimedia Commons.


Start with history, because the course’s chemistry was once a political argument. In the late 1960s Swedish scientists linked acidifying lakes in the west of Sweden to sulfur emissions from industrial regions elsewhere in Europe. Sweden presented this as its case study at the 1972 Stockholm conference. Many were sceptical, and it took years of measurements of deposition, soils and lake chemistry before the case was accepted. The 1979 Convention on Long-Range Transboundary Air Pollution followed. Last lecture we followed organic contaminants through phases. Today we put every thread together for one constructed catchment, and practise building a chemical explanation that could convince a sceptic.

About this lecture


In 1967 the Swedish soil scientist Svante Odén argued in a newspaper article that Scandinavian lakes and soils were being acidified by sulfur emitted in other countries, carried hundreds of kilometres by the wind. At the 1972 UN environment conference in Stockholm, Sweden presented the case. In 1979 it led to the first international air-pollution treaty.
Today you will learn to:

follow SO₂ and NOₓ as they are oxidised to acids;
separate wet and dry deposition and compute a flux;
count acid equivalents, and see why sulfate alone is not an acid dose;
build an annual catchment budget.

Last lecture: OH and sunlight. Today: what OH does to SO₂ and NO₂.

 Bełchatów coal power station, Poland, one of the largest in Europe. Photo: Petr Štefek, CC BY 2.5, Wikimedia Commons.


Open with the Swedish story, because acid rain is where environmental chemistry became international politics. In the 1960s Svante Odén connected acidifying lakes in Scandinavia with emissions from Britain and central Europe, using data from a network of precipitation samplers. Sweden brought the case to the 1972 United Nations conference in Stockholm, and in 1979 the Convention on Long-range Transboundary Air Pollution was signed. Its later protocols, including the 1999 Gothenburg Protocol, cut emissions across Europe. Today’s chemical question: how does a gas leaving a chimney become an acid in a lake, and why does measuring the pH of rain not tell us how much acid a catchment receives?

Back to the rain sample


 Precipitation sampler of the NADP Mercury Deposition Network. Photo: Mary O’Neill, USGS, public domain, Wikimedia Commons.

A rain sample contains sulfate. Before reporting a strong-acid dose, you need:

the acidity (H⁺) or alkalinity of the sample;
the major cations, including NH₄⁺, Ca²⁺, Na⁺, Mg²⁺;
the other anions (NO₃⁻, Cl⁻);
the precipitation volume, to turn concentration into flux;
an estimate of dry deposition;
what happens after deposition: nitrification, uptake, weathering.



This is exercise 4 and the opening question of the lecture. The answer collects every idea from today. Sulfate tells you the anion. Measured acidity tells you the free hydrogen ion now. The cations tell you whether sulfate came as acid, as ammonium salt or as sea salt or dust. The volume converts concentration to an amount per square metre. Dry deposition is missing from the sample by design. And the catchment processes decide how much of the deposited acidity actually reaches the lake. A careful report states which of these were measured and which were assumed. That is the same discipline as closing a mass balance in lecture 1.

About this lecture


In parts of Skåne and on Gotland, where the bedrock contains limestone, tap water is hard. Kettles there grow a white crust within weeks. In much of northern Sweden, on granite, they hardly scale at all.
The crust is calcite, CaCO₃: the same mineral that liming adds to lakes. Why does it come out of the water when you boil it?
Today you will learn to:

use Henry’s law to connect dissolved CO₂ to the air;
explain why CO₂ uptake raises CT but not alkalinity, unlike strong acid;
judge mineral stability with the saturation state Ω;
separate what is favoured from what is fast.


 Limescale (CaCO₃) from a kettle. Photo: Alexander Davronov, CC BY-SA 4.0, Wikimedia Commons.


Pick up exactly where the last lecture stopped. We ended with two open questions: what happens when the water meets the air, and why does a kettle scale up? We also saw that carbon dioxide uptake by the ocean is not the same as adding strong acid. Today we answer all three. The kettle is a good hook because every student has seen limescale, and the same chemistry builds stalactites, tufa waterfalls and the shells of marine plankton. Point out that the regional difference in Sweden is the same bedrock story as the lakes last time: limestone supplies calcium and alkalinity, granite does not.

About this lecture


Over recent decades, many lakes and rivers in Sweden and the rest of northern Europe have become browner. Researchers call it brownification. Water utilities notice it first: browner raw water needs more treatment before it can be drunk.
The colour comes from dissolved organic matter washed in from forest soils and peat.
Today you will learn to:

read what DOC measures, and what it does not;
explain how organic acids bind protons and metals;
connect colour to light in the water;
calculate an idealised oxygen demand and say when water runs out of oxygen.


 Brown, humic river water, Korkeakoskenjoki, Finland. Photo: Anna Reponen, CC BY 4.0, Wikimedia Commons.


Open with brownification because it is happening in the students’ own landscape. Long-term monitoring across Scandinavia, Finland and the UK has shown rising water colour and dissolved organic carbon in many lakes and rivers since the 1980s and 1990s. Several causes have been proposed, and we return to them at the end. Last lecture we followed inorganic carbon between air, water and calcite. Organic carbon is the other large pool of carbon in fresh water, and in boreal lakes it is often larger than the inorganic pool. It also explains a fact from last time: many northern lakes are supersaturated with carbon dioxide, partly because organic matter from land is decomposed in the water.

Closing the balance in the field


 USGS team taking baseline water and sediment samples at Gulf Shores, Alabama, before oil from the 2010 Deepwater Horizon spill arrived. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, public domain, Wikimedia Commons.

A lake’s concentration falls while its volume rises. To tell dilution, export and transformation apart, measure over the same period:

volume and total inventory in the water;
inflow and outflow loads;
amounts in sediment, biota or air;
likely transformation products.

A concentration time series alone cannot close the balance.


This is exercise 4. The general lesson is that a closed balance needs several kinds of measurement, collected over a consistent period. Volume and inventory tell you whether the amount actually changed. Loads at the inlet and outlet tell you about export. Solid phases tell you about transfer. Products are the positive evidence for transformation. If any of these are missing, say which explanation remains open. The photo shows routine field sampling; field teams plan exactly this: which compartments and which species, at which times. In later units we will add sorption, redox reactions and biodegradation, but the bookkeeping stays the same.
